/* AbiLearn — Social Science MCQ Bank — History Ch 11, 12, 13 (150 Questions) */

// CH11_SOCIAL (History Ch3 — The Making of a Global World)
const SOCIAL_CH11 = [
  { q: 'Which ancient trade route connected China to the Mediterranean world, facilitating the exchange of silk, spices, and ideas?', opts: ['Silk Routes', 'Spice Route', 'Trans-Saharan Route', 'Amber Road'], ans: 0, exp: 'The Silk Routes were a network of overland and sea routes linking China to the Mediterranean. They were not just conduits for silk but also carried spices, textiles, precious metals, and transmitted religions like Buddhism, Islam, and Christianity across continents.' },
  { q: 'Which of the following best describes the significance of the Silk Routes for cultural exchange?', opts: ['They only allowed one-way trade from East to West', 'They facilitated the spread of Buddhism, Islam, and Christianity alongside commodities', 'They were exclusively sea routes controlled by Arab merchants', 'They were established only after the 15th century CE'], ans: 1, exp: 'The Silk Routes were multi-directional cultural corridors. Missionaries, pilgrims, and merchants carried religious ideas alongside goods. Buddhism spread from India to Central and East Asia, while Christianity and Islam later followed similar paths.' },
  { q: 'Before Columbus\'s 1492 voyage, which crop was entirely unknown to the Old World yet later became a staple food in Ireland and parts of Europe?', opts: ['Rice', 'Wheat', 'Potato', 'Maize'], ans: 2, exp: 'The potato (along with maize, tomatoes, chilies, and groundnuts) originated in the Americas and was completely unknown in Europe, Africa, and Asia before 1492. The potato later became so central to Irish diet that the Great Famine of 1840s devastated Ireland when the crop failed.' },
  { q: 'The conquest of the Americas by Spain and Portugal was made significantly easier by which biological factor?', opts: ['Superior European weaponry alone', 'The alliance of all indigenous tribes with Europeans', 'The voluntary surrender of Aztec and Inca rulers', 'Diseases like smallpox that devastated indigenous populations with no immunity'], ans: 3, exp: 'European colonisers unknowingly (and sometimes knowingly) carried diseases like smallpox to which indigenous Americans had no immunity. Historians estimate that smallpox and other diseases wiped out up to 90% of indigenous American populations, fatally weakening resistance to conquest.' },
  { q: 'Approximately what percentage of the indigenous population of the Americas was wiped out by diseases brought by European colonisers after 1492?', opts: ['90%', '20%', '50%', '70%'], ans: 0, exp: 'It is estimated that diseases like smallpox, measles, and influenza — to which indigenous Americans had no prior immunity — killed approximately 90% of the native population of the Americas in the decades following European contact, transforming the demographic and political landscape.' },
  { q: 'The repeal of the Corn Laws in Britain in 1846 had which primary economic consequence?', opts: ['It raised bread prices and caused urban starvation', 'It allowed cheap food imports, lowering urban living costs and enabling workers to buy manufactured goods', 'It helped British farmers by eliminating competition', 'It was opposed by industrialists who wanted food prices to rise'], ans: 1, exp: 'The Corn Laws kept grain prices artificially high by restricting imports. When repealed in 1846, cheaper food could be imported, reducing urban workers\' food costs. This freed up income for manufactured goods and signalled Britain\'s commitment to free trade, triggering large-scale agricultural imports from America and Australia.' },
  { q: 'Which of the following best describes the system of "indentured labour" introduced by the British after the abolition of slavery?', opts: ['Voluntary migration with full workers\' rights and freedom to return', 'A government programme paying workers a living wage overseas', 'A contract system where workers were bound for 5 years with severe penalties for breach, resembling bonded labour', 'A scheme restricted to European workers in British colonies'], ans: 2, exp: 'Indentured labour (1830–1920) involved workers signing 5-year contracts to work on plantations in the Caribbean, Fiji, Mauritius, and other colonies. They had no freedom to leave before the contract ended, were paid very little, and faced corporal punishment. Critics called it a "new system of slavery."' },
  { q: 'The indentured labourers recruited to work in Caribbean plantations were primarily from which regions of India?', opts: ['Punjab and Rajasthan', 'Bengal and Assam', 'Tamil Nadu and Kerala only', 'Eastern UP, Bihar, and central India'], ans: 3, exp: 'Most indentured workers came from the poor, drought-prone districts of eastern Uttar Pradesh, Bihar, central India, and parts of Tamil Nadu, where peasants had been dispossessed of land due to the commercialisation of agriculture under colonialism, forcing them into desperate migration.' },
  { q: 'What was the role of the "arkatti" in the system of indentured labour migration?', opts: ['A local recruiter who often lured or deceived poor workers into signing indenture contracts', 'A British plantation manager who supervised workers', 'A government official who verified the legality of labour contracts', 'A community leader who organised protest against indenture'], ans: 0, exp: 'The arkatti was a sub-agent or local recruiter hired by labour contractors to find workers in villages. They often misrepresented conditions in the colonies, promising high wages and easy work, effectively trapping poor and ignorant labourers into harsh indenture contracts far from home.' },
  { q: 'Between 1834 and 1920, millions of indentured labourers were transported to which of the following sets of colonies to work on plantations?', opts: ['Canada, Australia, New Zealand', 'Trinidad, Guyana, Fiji, Mauritius, Ceylon', 'Egypt, Sudan, South Africa', 'Burma, Malaya, Indonesia'], ans: 1, exp: 'After the abolition of slavery in 1833, the British transported indentured workers primarily to Trinidad, British Guiana (Guyana), Jamaica, Fiji, Mauritius, Ceylon (Sri Lanka), and Malaya to maintain plantation labour. These communities form the basis of South Asian diaspora in those regions.' },
  { q: 'Which Indian nationalist leader described the indentured labour system as a "girmit" and campaigned vigorously for its abolition?', opts: ['B.R. Ambedkar', 'Bal Gangadhar Tilak', 'Mahatma Gandhi', 'Gopal Krishna Gokhale'], ans: 2, exp: 'Mahatma Gandhi, who had witnessed Indian indentured labour conditions in South Africa, called indenture a "girmit" (a corruption of "agreement") — a condition of semi-slavery. His campaigns, combined with nationalist pressure, led to the abolition of the indenture system in 1917.' },
  { q: 'The 19th century world economy was characterised by which phenomenon linked to food and raw material trade?', opts: ['Balanced trade between all regions', 'Colonies having economic sovereignty over their resources', 'Asia dominating world manufacturing throughout the 1800s', 'Core industrial nations exporting manufactured goods and importing food/raw materials from peripheral regions'], ans: 3, exp: 'The 19th-century global economy created an unequal structure: industrial core nations (Britain, France, Germany, USA) exported manufactured goods and imported cheap food and raw materials from peripheral/colonial regions like India, Africa, and Latin America — a pattern that reinforced colonial dependency.' },
  { q: 'How did the construction of railways in colonies like India affect global trade patterns in the 19th century?', opts: ['Railways connected interior regions to ports, dramatically expanding the export of food grains and raw materials', 'Railways reduced agricultural output in India', 'Railways were built exclusively for military use with no economic impact', 'Railways benefited Indian merchants primarily over British traders'], ans: 0, exp: 'Railways penetrated India\'s agricultural hinterland, allowing cheap and rapid movement of cash crops (cotton, indigo, jute, wheat) from interior districts to coastal ports. This vastly expanded India\'s role as a supplier of raw materials in the global economy, often at the expense of food security for local populations.' },
  { q: 'The Great Depression of 1929 was triggered immediately by which event?', opts: ['The assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand', 'The Wall Street Crash (stock market collapse) in October 1929', 'The collapse of the British Pound Sterling', 'A major drought destroying American crops'], ans: 1, exp: 'The Great Depression began with the catastrophic crash of the New York Stock Exchange (Wall Street) in October 1929. Overspeculation, buying on margin, and inflated asset values created a bubble that burst, wiping out billions of dollars of wealth and triggering a cascading financial crisis.' },
  { q: 'Which of the following was NOT a direct cause or feature of the Great Depression (1929–1932)?', opts: ['Mass bank failures in the United States', 'Collapse of agricultural prices globally', 'A sudden rise in industrial wages across Europe', 'Sharp decline in world trade due to protectionist tariffs'], ans: 2, exp: 'Industrial wages actually fell sharply during the Depression as factories closed and unemployment rose. The Depression was characterised by bank failures, credit collapse, agricultural deflation, massive unemployment, and protectionist trade wars (like the US Smoot-Hawley Tariff Act of 1930) that further contracted world trade.' },
  { q: 'At the peak of the Great Depression in the United States (around 1932–33), the unemployment rate reached approximately:', opts: ['5–8%', '12–15%', '40–50%', '25–33%'], ans: 3, exp: 'At its worst (1932–33), US unemployment reached approximately 25–33% — meaning about one in four workers had no job. Industrial output fell by nearly 50%, thousands of banks collapsed, and millions were reduced to poverty, fundamentally shaking confidence in capitalism.' },
  { q: 'President Franklin D. Roosevelt\'s "New Deal" was primarily designed to:', opts: ['Restore confidence, create employment through public works, and regulate financial markets', 'Increase military spending to end the Depression through war production', 'Privatise all government services to stimulate the economy', 'Abandon the gold standard and adopt a barter system'], ans: 0, exp: 'Roosevelt\'s New Deal (from 1933) was a set of programmes, reforms, and regulations including the Works Progress Administration (public works), Social Security Act, banking reforms (Glass-Steagall Act), farm support schemes, and financial regulations, aimed at relief, recovery, and reform.' },
  { q: 'Which of the following New Deal programmes directly employed millions of unemployed Americans in public construction projects?', opts: ['Federal Reserve Act', 'Works Progress Administration (WPA)', 'Sherman Antitrust Act', 'Monroe Doctrine'], ans: 1, exp: 'The Works Progress Administration (later Work Projects Administration), established in 1935, employed millions in building roads, bridges, public buildings, parks, and arts projects. It was the largest New Deal agency, providing work relief that preserved skills and dignity while building public infrastructure.' },
  { q: 'The Bretton Woods Conference of 1944, which shaped the post-war international economic order, was held in which US state?', opts: ['New York', 'Washington DC', 'New Hampshire', 'Massachusetts'], ans: 2, exp: 'The Bretton Woods Conference (officially the United Nations Monetary and Financial Conference) was held in July 1944 in Bretton Woods, New Hampshire. It established the framework for post-war international economic cooperation, creating the IMF and World Bank.' },
  { q: 'The primary purpose of the International Monetary Fund (IMF), established after Bretton Woods, was to:', opts: ['Fund long-term infrastructure projects in developing countries', 'Control global oil prices and energy supply', 'Regulate international stock exchanges', 'Manage short-term balance of payments crises by providing temporary loans to member nations'], ans: 3, exp: 'The IMF was designed to manage short-term currency and balance-of-payments crises, preventing the kind of competitive devaluations and trade wars that had worsened the Great Depression. It provides emergency loans to countries facing financial crises, subject to economic reform conditions.' },
  { q: 'The World Bank (International Bank for Reconstruction and Development), established at Bretton Woods, was initially created to:', opts: ['Finance long-term post-war reconstruction and development projects', 'Regulate world currency exchange rates', 'Administer the Marshall Plan for European recovery', 'Manage the debts of wartime nations'], ans: 0, exp: 'The World Bank (IBRD) was established to provide long-term loans for post-war reconstruction of war-damaged Europe and later for development of newly independent nations. It differs from the IMF in focus: long-term development vs. short-term balance of payments.' },
  { q: 'The Bretton Woods system was based on which monetary arrangement that gave the US dollar a central role in world trade?', opts: ['Free floating exchange rates with no fixed value', 'The gold-dollar standard: the US dollar pegged to gold at $35/ounce, other currencies pegged to the dollar', 'All currencies pegged to the British pound', 'A barter system replacing currency in international trade'], ans: 1, exp: 'The Bretton Woods system established a gold-dollar standard where the US dollar was convertible to gold at $35/ounce, and all other currencies maintained fixed exchange rates against the dollar. This gave the dollar reserve currency status and the US enormous influence over the global economy.' },
  { q: 'The General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade (GATT), established in 1948, aimed primarily to:', opts: ['Create a world government to manage trade disputes', 'Give developing countries preferential access to Western markets', 'Reduce tariffs and trade barriers through multilateral negotiations', 'Nationalise key industries across member nations'], ans: 2, exp: 'GATT was a multilateral agreement to reduce tariffs and non-tariff barriers to international trade through successive rounds of negotiations. It evolved into the World Trade Organization (WTO) in 1995, and its principles of non-discrimination and reciprocal tariff reduction shaped modern trade policy.' },
  { q: 'What was the significance of the "Bretton Woods twins" for developing countries newly independent after World War II?', opts: ['They provided grants to ex-colonies to build democratic institutions', 'They banned developing countries from borrowing until 1980', 'They transferred technology from developed to developing nations freely', 'They offered loans for development but often attached conditions that constrained economic sovereignty'], ans: 3, exp: 'The IMF and World Bank (the "Bretton Woods twins") did provide loans for development, but their structural adjustment conditions — requiring privatisation, fiscal austerity, and market liberalisation — often constrained the policy autonomy of developing nations and were controversial in their impact on poverty.' },
  { q: 'Which term describes the phenomenon where multinational corporations from wealthy countries set up production in poor countries to take advantage of cheap labour and lax regulations?', opts: ['Globalisation through foreign direct investment', 'Fair trade', 'Mercantilism', 'Colonialism'], ans: 0, exp: 'Modern globalisation involves MNCs investing in developing countries (foreign direct investment) to access cheaper labour, raw materials, or new markets. Critics argue this can exploit workers and environment in host countries; proponents see it as generating employment and technology transfer.' },
  { q: 'The post-World War II economic recovery of Western Europe and Japan is often attributed to:', opts: ['Complete free market policies with no government intervention', 'The Marshall Plan, US aid, Bretton Woods institutions, and high government investment', 'Reparations received from defeated Germany and Japan', 'Soviet economic assistance to Western European nations'], ans: 1, exp: 'Western Europe\'s recovery was driven by massive US aid (Marshall Plan, ~$13 billion), IMF/World Bank support, Keynesian economic management (high public investment), and the economic stimulus of rebuilding war-damaged infrastructure. Japan\'s recovery was similarly supported by US aid and strategic economic planning.' },
  { q: 'Which of the following arguments is typically used by critics of economic globalisation?', opts: ['Globalisation creates equal benefits for all nations', 'Free trade always protects workers\' rights', 'Globalisation increases inequality, makes developing nations dependent, and undermines local cultures', 'MNCs always pay higher wages than local employers'], ans: 2, exp: 'Critics of globalisation argue it has increased inequality within and between nations, created dependency relationships (developing countries remain commodity exporters), led to exploitation of cheap labour, undermined local industries, and homogenised cultures. These arguments have fuelled fair trade movements and anti-globalisation protests.' },
  { q: 'The concept of a pre-modern world economy being "multi-polar" refers to:', opts: ['Many countries having nuclear weapons', 'The existence of multiple stock exchanges before 1800', 'Several European empires dividing the world between them', 'Asia (especially India and China) accounting for three-fourths of world production before the Industrial Revolution'], ans: 3, exp: 'Historians estimate that before European industrialisation, India and China together accounted for 50–75% of global economic output. The shift toward a Eurocentric global economy was a product of 19th-century industrialisation and colonialism, not an ancient reality.' },
  { q: 'Cowrie shells were used as currency in large parts of Africa and South Asia. What does this tell us about pre-modern trade networks?', opts: ['Pre-modern trade networks were extensive, connecting the Indian Ocean, East Africa, and the Americas', 'Trade did not exist before European colonisation', 'Cowrie shells were only used locally with no long-distance significance', 'European merchants introduced cowrie shells to Africa'], ans: 0, exp: 'Cowrie shells from the Maldive Islands circulated widely as currency in South Asian, African, and even some American trade networks centuries before European expansion. Their distribution reveals sophisticated pre-modern Indian Ocean trade routes and monetary systems.' },
  { q: 'The term "proto-globalisation" in the context of pre-1500 history refers to:', opts: ['European efforts to dominate world trade after Columbus', 'Long-distance trade connections that existed across Eurasia and Africa centuries before European expansion', 'The trade agreements signed between medieval European states', 'The Roman Empire\'s control of Mediterranean trade'], ans: 1, exp: 'Proto-globalisation refers to the extensive, multi-directional trade networks — Silk Routes, Indian Ocean trade, Trans-Saharan routes — that connected civilisations across Eurasia and Africa long before European expansion. These were sophisticated commercial systems, not primitive barter exchanges.' },
  { q: 'Which of the following crops, originally from the Americas, contributed most significantly to European population growth after the 16th century?', opts: ['Wheat', 'Rice', 'Potato and Maize', 'Barley'], ans: 2, exp: 'The potato and maize, introduced from the Americas after 1492, dramatically increased caloric yields per acre in Europe, enabling population growth. The potato could feed a family on a small plot — which made Ireland\'s later dependence on it catastrophic when potato blight struck in the 1840s.' },
  { q: 'The term "mercantilism" as a trade philosophy, dominant in Europe from the 16th to 18th centuries, emphasised:', opts: ['Free trade between all nations without restrictions', 'Equal distribution of wealth across colonies and the mother country', 'Encouraging colonies to industrialise and export manufactured goods', 'Accumulating gold and silver through a favourable trade balance and protecting domestic industries'], ans: 3, exp: 'Mercantilism held that national wealth depended on accumulating precious metals (gold/silver) through a trade surplus: exporting more than importing. Colonial systems were designed to provide raw materials cheaply to the mother country and buy its manufactured goods, preventing colonies from industrialising.' },
  { q: 'Which of the following best explains why the Great Depression spread from the US to the rest of the world so rapidly?', opts: ['The interconnected nature of global financial markets, trade, and the gold standard transmitted the crisis internationally', 'Military alliances forced all nations to adopt US economic policies', 'Other nations deliberately adopted the Depression to weaken the US', 'The Depression only affected countries that had participated in World War I'], ans: 0, exp: 'The Depression spread globally through interconnected financial systems: US bank failures contracted credit globally; US tariffs under the Smoot-Hawley Act provoked retaliatory tariffs reducing world trade by 65%; the gold standard forced deflationary policies on countries; and US loans to Europe were recalled, collapsing European banks.' },
  { q: 'India\'s experience during the Great Depression was characterised by:', opts: ['Rapid industrialisation as foreign investment poured in', 'Falling agricultural prices, peasant indebtedness, falling exports, and decline in artisan incomes', 'A boom as Indian exports filled the gap left by US production', 'No impact due to India\'s self-sufficient village economy'], ans: 1, exp: 'India suffered severely: agricultural prices collapsed (wheat fell by 50%), peasants could not repay loans and were ruined, exports of raw materials fell drastically, Indian artisans faced competition from cheap British goods, and the colonial government\'s insistence on continuing gold shipments to Britain drained India\'s resources.' },
  { q: 'The post-1945 Bretton Woods system represented a compromise between which two economic philosophies?', opts: ['Communism and feudalism', 'Pure laissez-faire capitalism with no government intervention', 'Keynesian state management with embedded liberalism — open markets but with welfare state protection', 'Autarky (economic self-sufficiency) for all nations'], ans: 2, exp: 'Bretton Woods embodied "embedded liberalism" — open international trade and investment (liberalism) but embedded in domestic frameworks of welfare states, Keynesian management, and social protection. This allowed nations to pursue full employment while participating in an open international economy, avoiding the inter-war policy failures.' },
  { q: 'Which of the following is a key difference between 19th century globalisation and post-1990 globalisation?', opts: ['19th century globalisation involved only trade; post-1990 globalisation is only about culture', '19th century was led by developing nations; post-1990 by developed nations', 'There is no significant difference between the two periods', '19th century involved primarily capital and goods flows; post-1990 includes far greater integration of financial markets, technology, and information flows'], ans: 3, exp: 'While 19th-century globalisation involved significant goods trade and some capital flows (especially portfolio investment in colonial infrastructure), post-1990 globalisation is characterised by instantaneous global financial flows, global production chains, digital information exchange, and much greater integration of service sectors.' },
  { q: 'The "Corn Laws" in early 19th-century Britain protected the interests of which group?', opts: ['Landed aristocracy and large farmers who benefited from high grain prices', 'Urban industrial workers who needed cheap grain', 'Merchant traders importing grain from America', 'Colonial planters in the Caribbean'], ans: 0, exp: 'The Corn Laws (1815) imposed tariffs on imported grain to keep domestic grain prices high. This benefited the landed aristocracy and large farmers (Tory political base) at the expense of urban workers and industrialists who wanted cheap food. The Anti-Corn Law League led the successful campaign for repeal in 1846.' },
  { q: 'After the repeal of the Corn Laws in 1846, Britain\'s agricultural sector responded by:', opts: ['Rapidly modernising and increasing efficiency to compete with imports', 'Collapsing as imports flooded in from the Americas and Australia, leaving land uncultivated', 'Switching to food exports as British farms became the most productive in the world', 'Being protected by new hidden subsidies that replaced the Corn Laws'], ans: 1, exp: 'After repeal, cheap grain imports from the fertile prairies of the Americas (enabled by railways and steamships) flooded Britain, making British farming uncompetitive. Large areas of arable land were abandoned, farm labourers migrated to cities or emigrated, and Britain became heavily dependent on imported food.' },
  { q: 'The period 1870–1914 saw a massive expansion of European investment overseas. Where did most of this investment flow?', opts: ['Africa only', 'Asian manufacturing industries', 'USA, Canada, Australia, Latin America — building railways, ports, and infrastructure', 'The Ottoman Empire and Central Asia exclusively'], ans: 2, exp: 'Between 1870–1914, European (especially British) capital flowed massively into settler colonies and Latin America, financing railways, ports, mines, and plantations. By 1914, Britain had invested about £4 billion overseas. This investment integrated global commodity markets but also created debt dependency in recipient countries.' },
  { q: 'What does the textbook mean when it says that 19th-century globalisation created a world of "unequal partners"?', opts: ['Some countries were richer than others for purely geographic reasons', 'Developing nations chose not to industrialise', 'Colonial subjects willingly traded on unequal terms', 'Industrial nations set the terms of trade, determining prices of raw materials and manufactured goods to their advantage, leaving colonies and periphery nations at a disadvantage'], ans: 3, exp: 'The 19th-century global economy was structured by colonial power: industrial nations controlled prices, shipping routes, insurance, and financial services. Colonies and periphery nations were "price takers" — locked into exporting cheap raw materials and importing expensive manufactured goods — a structural inequality that persisted into the 20th century.' },
  { q: 'Which of the following BEST explains why the abolition of slavery led to the introduction of indentured labour?', opts: ['Both A and B are correct', 'Former slaves demanded higher wages that planters could not afford, creating a labour shortage on plantations', 'The British government needed a legal alternative to maintain plantation profitability', 'Indentured labour was introduced before the abolition of slavery'], ans: 0, exp: 'Abolition of slavery in 1833 freed enslaved people, many of whom refused to work on plantations under any conditions given the trauma of slavery. This created genuine labour shortages. Planters lobbied the British government for an alternative, leading to the indentured system that maintained cheap labour under legally sanctioned contracts.' },
  { q: 'The cultural significance of indentured labour communities is seen today in which of the following?', opts: ['All indentured workers returned to India after contracts ended', 'Indentured communities created hybrid cultures — blending Indian, African, and Creole traditions — visible in music (chutney-soca), food, and festivals in Trinidad, Fiji, and Mauritius', 'Indentured workers were assimilated completely into British culture', 'Indentured communities formed no lasting cultural identity'], ans: 1, exp: 'Indentured communities, cut off from their home regions and thrown together across caste and linguistic lines, created entirely new hybrid cultures. Chutney-soca music in Trinidad, specific forms of Hinduism in Fiji, and unique culinary traditions in Mauritius all testify to this cultural creativity forged under adversity.' },
  { q: 'The "Marshall Plan" of 1948 was significant for post-war globalisation because:', opts: ['It created the United Nations', 'It established the Bretton Woods monetary system', 'It provided $13 billion in US aid to rebuild Western European economies, creating markets for US goods and containing communism', 'It formed NATO as a military alliance'], ans: 2, exp: 'The European Recovery Programme (Marshall Plan) provided massive US financial aid to 17 Western European nations from 1948 to 1952. It accelerated European recovery, created markets for US exports, integrated European economies, and served as a geopolitical strategy to prevent Soviet expansion — combining economic and Cold War objectives.' },
  { q: 'What was the significance of the telegraph and steamship in the 19th-century global economy?', opts: ['They were purely military technologies with no economic impact', 'They benefited only European nations and had no impact on colonies', 'They replaced sail ships and post horses but otherwise changed little in trade patterns', 'They dramatically reduced communication time and transportation costs, making global trade more integrated and predictable'], ans: 3, exp: 'The telegraph enabled instantaneous international communication (prices, news, orders), while steamships made ocean transport faster, cheaper, and more reliable. Together they shrank the "tyranny of distance," enabling merchants to manage global supply chains, insure shipments, and respond to price signals across continents — creating truly integrated world markets.' },
  { q: 'Which event of 1944 is considered the foundation of the post-war international economic architecture?', opts: ['The Bretton Woods Conference establishing the IMF and World Bank', 'The D-Day invasion at Normandy', 'The Yalta Conference dividing Europe', 'The signing of the Atlantic Charter'], ans: 0, exp: 'The Bretton Woods Conference (July 1944, New Hampshire) created the institutional architecture of the post-war economy: the IMF for monetary stability, the World Bank for reconstruction and development, and a fixed exchange rate system. It reflected the determination of Allied powers to avoid the inter-war financial chaos that had contributed to World War II.' },
  { q: 'Thomas Friedman\'s metaphor of the "flat world" in the context of globalisation suggests:', opts: ['The earth is literally flat according to some economists', 'Technology and trade liberalisation have created a level playing field where anyone can compete globally', 'Poor countries have overcome all disadvantages in global trade', 'Globalisation is a temporary phenomenon that will end soon'], ans: 1, exp: 'Friedman\'s "flat world" metaphor argues that internet technology, global supply chains, and trade liberalisation have reduced geographical and economic barriers, allowing companies and individuals from any country to compete globally. Critics counter that the world remains "spiky" — inequality persists in accessing these opportunities.' },
  { q: 'The "Gold Standard" that underpinned 19th-century global trade required countries experiencing trade deficits to:', opts: ['Print more money to pay off debts', 'Borrow from the IMF to maintain reserves', 'Ship gold out, contracting the money supply, causing deflation and recession to restore balance', 'Devalue their currencies freely to improve competitiveness'], ans: 2, exp: 'Under the Gold Standard, currencies were convertible to gold at fixed rates. A deficit country lost gold reserves, shrinking the money supply, forcing price deflation, wage cuts, and often recession — which ultimately restored export competitiveness. This mechanism was brutal for workers and was one reason countries abandoned gold during the Depression.' },
  { q: 'The NCERT textbook states that between 1820 and 1914 world trade grew nearly 25-fold. Which factor was LEAST responsible for this growth?', opts: ['Steam-powered transport reducing shipping costs', 'Spread of colonial rule opening new markets', 'Technological improvements in agricultural production enabling surplus exports', 'Abolition of slavery creating new consumer demand in colonies'], ans: 3, exp: 'While emancipation of enslaved people was morally significant, former slaves remained extremely poor and created minimal new consumer demand. The primary drivers of 19th-century trade expansion were technological (steamships, telegraphs, railways), institutional (free trade policies after Corn Law repeal), and structural (colonialism forcing raw material exports).' },
  { q: 'Which of the following best describes the argument that globalisation is not a new phenomenon?', opts: ['Long-distance trade, migration, and cultural exchange have existed for millennia, though their scale, speed, and impact have intensified since the 19th century', 'Modern globalisation and ancient trade are essentially identical in scale and character', 'Globalisation only became meaningful after the internet was invented', 'Only the last 30 years count as true globalisation due to WTO membership'], ans: 0, exp: 'Historians argue globalisation has deep roots: the Silk Routes connected Eurasia for centuries; the Indian Ocean was a busy trade highway before European dominance; the Americas were integrated into global trade after 1492. What changed after industrialisation was the scale, speed, depth of integration, and the asymmetry of power between nations.' },
  { q: 'What does the term "de-globalisation" refer to in the post-2008 context?', opts: ['The complete cessation of international trade', 'A trend of declining cross-border trade, investment, and financial flows relative to GDP after the 2008 financial crisis, accompanied by rising protectionism', 'Countries voluntarily isolating themselves like North Korea', 'The collapse of the WTO and all trade agreements'], ans: 1, exp: 'After the 2008 global financial crisis, world trade growth slowed significantly, cross-border financial flows contracted, and political movements in several countries (Brexit, US tariff wars) signalled a retreat from globalisation. Economists call this "slowbalisation" or de-globalisation — a questioning of the neoliberal consensus that had driven post-1990 integration.' },
];

// CH12_SOCIAL (History Ch4 — The Age of Industrialisation)
const SOCIAL_CH12 = [
  { q: 'What does the term "proto-industrialisation" refer to in the context of European economic history?', opts: ['A phase of large-scale industrial production before factories, organised through the domestic/putting-out system in rural areas', 'The period of rapid factory-based industrialisation in 18th-century Britain', 'The industrialisation of agricultural techniques using machinery', 'State-owned manufacturing before the rise of private capitalism'], ans: 0, exp: 'Proto-industrialisation refers to the pre-factory phase of industrial production (17th–18th centuries) where merchant capitalists supplied raw materials to rural households, who processed them at home and returned finished goods. This "putting-out" or domestic system spread production across the countryside without centralising it in factories.' },
  { q: 'In the putting-out system of proto-industrialisation, who provided the capital, raw materials, and market connections?', opts: ['The cottage workers who owned the tools of production', 'Merchant capitalists who distributed raw materials and collected finished products', 'The state, which organised production through guilds', 'Rural landlords who invested surplus agricultural profits'], ans: 1, exp: 'In the putting-out (domestic) system, merchant capitalists supplied raw materials (wool, cotton, metals) to rural workers, who processed them at home using their own tools. The merchants collected finished goods, marketed them, and controlled the entire value chain — the workers merely provided labour in their own homes.' },
  { q: 'Which invention by James Hargreaves in 1764 dramatically accelerated cotton spinning?', opts: ['Water Frame', 'Steam Engine', 'Spinning Jenny', 'Power Loom'], ans: 2, exp: 'James Hargreaves invented the Spinning Jenny in 1764. It allowed a single worker to spin multiple threads simultaneously (the jenny could spin 6–8 threads at once, later models more), multiplying productivity enormously compared to the single-thread spinning wheel, and accelerating Britain\'s cotton textile revolution.' },
  { q: 'James Watt\'s improved steam engine, patented in 1769 and made commercially viable by the 1780s, transformed British industry primarily because:', opts: ['It was cheaper than water power in all locations', 'It was used exclusively for transportation and had no role in manufacturing', 'It eliminated the need for human labour immediately', 'It provided a reliable, controllable, and moveable power source independent of rivers, enabling factories anywhere'], ans: 3, exp: 'Watt\'s steam engine (improved from Newcomen\'s design) could be built anywhere, not just near fast-flowing rivers. This geographical freedom, combined with its controllable power output, enabled the expansion of factories in cities, powered textile mills, iron foundries, and eventually railways — making it the central technology of the Industrial Revolution.' },
  { q: 'The cotton textile industry was the leading sector of Britain\'s early Industrial Revolution. By 1840, what percentage of Britain\'s total exports did cotton goods represent?', opts: ['50%', '10%', '25%', '75%'], ans: 0, exp: 'By 1840, cotton goods represented roughly 50% of Britain\'s total exports by value — an extraordinary concentration illustrating how the cotton industry had transformed the British economy. Lancashire\'s mills, supplied by American slave-grown cotton, produced cloth for global markets from Asia to Africa to the Americas.' },
  { q: 'Which of the following best describes the social conditions of child workers in early British factories?', opts: ['Children worked short hours and were protected by factory owners', 'Children as young as 5–6 worked 14–16 hour days in dangerous conditions, with physical punishment common', 'Child labour was banned from the outset of the Industrial Revolution', 'Children only worked in factories during school holidays'], ans: 1, exp: 'Early industrial capitalism relied heavily on child labour — children worked in mines, textile mills, and chimneys from as young as 5. Hours were brutally long (12–16 hours), conditions dangerous, and physical punishment routine. The Factory Act of 1833 began restricting child labour, but enforcement was slow and incomplete for decades.' },
  { q: 'The Luddite movement of 1811–12 in Britain was a response to:', opts: ['Demands for universal male suffrage', 'Protest against the Corn Laws raising food prices', 'Skilled textile workers\' fear that labour-saving machinery would destroy their livelihoods and organised resistance through machine-breaking', 'Workers demanding higher wages without objecting to machines'], ans: 2, exp: 'Luddism arose from skilled textile workers (framework knitters in Nottinghamshire, woollen croppers in Yorkshire) who faced unemployment as merchants introduced labour-saving machinery. They organised, sometimes under the mythical General Ned Ludd, to smash new machines. The British government responded with the death penalty for machine-breaking.' },
  { q: 'Why did many industrialists in early 19th-century Britain prefer to hire women and children over adult men?', opts: ['Women and children were more skilled at industrial work', 'Labour laws required a minimum percentage of women and children in factories', 'Adult men refused to work in factories on principle', 'They could be paid significantly lower wages and were considered more docile and easier to discipline'], ans: 3, exp: 'Women and children were paid a fraction of adult male wages (often 25–50%) and were considered easier to discipline, less likely to unionise, and more suited to tasks requiring nimble fingers (like piecing broken threads). Their exploitation was central to keeping production costs low in competitive industries.' },
  { q: 'What was "seasonal unemployment" in the context of early British industrial factories?', opts: ['Fluctuations in demand meaning factories would operate at full capacity in busy seasons but lay off workers in slack periods, causing regular cycles of unemployment', 'Workers taking summer holidays voluntarily', 'Unemployment caused by agricultural seasons affecting rural workers only', 'The annual closure of factories for maintenance in winter'], ans: 0, exp: 'Many early industries (textiles, building, food processing) experienced seasonal demand fluctuations. Workers might work 14-hour days for months, then face complete unemployment in slack seasons. Without unemployment insurance or savings, this created acute poverty cycles and was a major source of working-class hardship in industrial cities.' },
  { q: 'The early British factory owners who opposed machinery — including some surprising voices — did so because:', opts: ['Machines never actually improved productivity', 'Some early capitalists worried machines would produce lower quality goods and disrupt stable piece-rate systems they relied on', 'The Church prohibited machine use in manufacturing', 'Foreign competition meant British goods did not need productivity improvements'], ans: 1, exp: 'Contrary to expectation, some early manufacturers resisted adopting new machinery because: skilled handcraft workers produced higher quality goods for luxury markets; machines required large capital investment with uncertain returns; disrupting existing putting-out networks risked losing experienced craft workers; and some markets valued artisanal quality over cheap machine production.' },
  { q: 'India was a major exporter of textiles to the world for centuries before colonialism. Which Indian textile products were most highly valued in global markets?', opts: ['Only coarse cotton cloth for ordinary use', 'Purely synthetic fabrics using early Indian chemistry', 'Fine muslin from Dhaka, painted chintz, and silk textiles that commanded premium prices in European and Asian markets', 'Only wool products from Rajasthan'], ans: 2, exp: 'India produced among the world\'s finest textiles: Dhaka muslin so fine it was called "woven air" (bafta, malmal), printed chintz (chint) from Gujarat and the Coromandel coast, Benaras brocades, Kashmiri shawls, and Odisha ikats. These were prized luxury goods in Europe and across Asia, making India the world\'s leading textile exporter before British deindustrialisation.' },
  { q: 'Which of the following best describes "deindustrialisation" as it occurred in India under British colonial rule?', opts: ['India voluntarily shifted from industry to agriculture for better economic returns', 'Industrialisation that moved from cities to rural areas', 'A natural economic evolution as India modernised', 'The systematic destruction of Indian artisanal industries (especially textiles) through colonial policies, cheap British machine-made imports, and withdrawal of court and aristocratic patronage'], ans: 3, exp: 'British deindustrialisation of India involved multiple mechanisms: Manchester machine-made textiles were imported duty-free into India while Indian goods faced tariffs in Britain; colonial revenue policies squeezed artisan incomes; the destruction of Mughal and princely courts removed wealthy patrons; and railways flooded interiors with cheap British goods, ruining local weavers and artisans.' },
  { q: 'The Dacca (Dhaka) muslin weavers faced a catastrophic decline in the 19th century. What was the approximate population of Dhaka at the peak of the muslin trade versus after its decline?', opts: ['Fell from approximately 150,000 in 1800 to about 30,000 by 1820', 'Remained unchanged at 100,000', 'Grew from 50,000 to 200,000 due to British investment', 'Fell from 1 million to 500,000 over 50 years'], ans: 0, exp: 'Dhaka\'s population fell dramatically — estimates suggest from approximately 150,000 at its height as a muslin-producing centre to perhaps 30,000 by the 1820s as the industry collapsed under British competition. This demographic collapse is one of the starkest illustrations of colonial deindustrialisation destroying entire urban economies.' },
  { q: 'Which of the following Indian merchants played a crucial role in financing the early Bombay cotton mills of the late 19th century?', opts: ['East India Company officials who invested profits', 'Parsi and Marwari merchants who had accumulated capital through trade in the colonial economy', 'British manufacturers who relocated to India', 'The princely states who provided industrial capital'], ans: 1, exp: 'Bombay\'s cotton mills (from the 1850s onward) were largely financed by Parsi merchants (like Cowasji Davar who founded the first mill in 1854) and later by Marwari traders. These communities had accumulated capital through opium trade, cotton export to China, and agency houses, and saw mill investment as the next frontier.' },
  { q: 'The "Manchester of India" refers to which city and why?', opts: ['Delhi, because of its ancient textile tradition', 'Bangalore, because of its modern IT industry', 'Ahmedabad, because of its concentration of cotton textile mills in the late 19th and early 20th centuries', 'Surat, because of the British East India Company\'s first Indian factory'], ans: 2, exp: 'Ahmedabad in Gujarat was nicknamed the "Manchester of India" for its concentration of cotton textile mills. By the early 20th century, Ahmedabad had dozens of large mills producing cotton cloth, exploiting nearby raw cotton supplies, cheap labour from rural migration, and access to railway networks.' },
  { q: 'One reason early Indian industrialists had difficulty competing with British goods despite lower Indian wages was:', opts: ['Indian workers were less productive than British workers', 'Indian consumers preferred British goods for cultural reasons', 'Indian raw materials were of lower quality than British ones', 'British industries benefited from superior technology, economies of scale, cheap coal, efficient transport, and state support that Indian industries lacked under colonial rule'], ans: 3, exp: 'British manufacturers had enormous structural advantages: mature technology, access to cheap coal, efficient rail and canal networks, established financial systems (banks, insurance), technical education institutions, and — crucially — colonial policies that favoured British goods in Indian markets while restricting Indian industrial development, making competition extremely difficult.' },
  { q: 'The "Factory Acts" in Britain during the 19th century were primarily aimed at:', opts: ['Regulating working conditions, limiting working hours, and restricting child and female labour', 'Increasing factory output through government incentives', 'Privatising state-owned industries', 'Encouraging workers to invest in the factories they worked in'], ans: 0, exp: 'A series of Factory Acts (1833, 1844, 1847, 1850, 1867) progressively regulated British industrial conditions: limiting children\'s working hours, requiring factory inspection, establishing minimum age requirements, capping adult women\'s hours, and eventually the Ten Hours Act (1847) limiting the working day. These resulted from working-class pressure and humanitarian reformers.' },
  { q: 'What does the NCERT text mean when it says that "not all workers were Luddites"?', opts: ['Some workers actively welcomed machinery as it made work easier', 'Workers were divided — some (especially skilled artisans threatened by machines) opposed them, while others (especially factory workers already employed by machines) saw employment benefits', 'Luddism affected only rural areas, not urban factories', 'Most workers were indifferent to industrialisation'], ans: 1, exp: 'Workers\' responses to industrialisation were not uniform. Skilled craft workers (handloom weavers, framework knitters) whose livelihoods were directly threatened by machines were most likely to resist. Factory workers who operated machines had different interests. Workers also organised differently — some through Luddism, others through early trade unions, Chartism, or cooperative movements.' },
  { q: 'The growth of the railways in Britain from the 1840s onward gave a massive boost to industrialisation primarily because:', opts: ['Railways mainly carried passengers, not goods', 'Railways replaced factories as the primary employment sector', 'They created huge demand for coal and iron, opened national markets, lowered transport costs, and generated enormous capital investment', 'They were built entirely with foreign capital'], ans: 2, exp: 'Railway construction created enormous "backward linkages": demand for coal, iron, steel, engineering goods, and lumber stimulated those industries. Railways opened national markets (reducing regional price differences), lowered transport costs enabling factory location far from markets, and the railway boom of the 1840s channelled vast capital into productive investment.' },
  { q: 'Before the factory system, why did merchants in the putting-out system prefer organising production in the countryside rather than in cities?', opts: ['Rural workers were more skilled than city artisans', 'Rural raw materials were cheaper and of higher quality', 'City governments taxed production more heavily than rural areas', 'Cities had powerful guilds that restricted production methods, wages, and who could produce; the countryside was outside guild jurisdiction'], ans: 3, exp: 'Urban guilds (of weavers, smiths, tanners etc.) exercised strict controls: they set wages, limited entry to trades, controlled production standards, and opposed innovation. Merchants who wanted to expand production and reduce costs went to the countryside where guild regulations did not apply, rural workers were desperate for income, and supervision was minimal.' },
  { q: 'Which of the following best describes the role of merchant capital in early industrialisation?', opts: ['Merchant capital provided the initial accumulation, trade networks, and market connections that financed and enabled the transition to factory production', 'Merchant capital was entirely replaced by industrial capital from the outset', 'Factory owners were entirely separate from the merchant class', 'Merchant capital actively opposed industrialisation to protect existing trade profits'], ans: 0, exp: 'Merchants who had accumulated capital through trade (in textiles, spices, colonial goods) provided the initial financing for early factories, brought knowledge of markets and supply chains, and in many cases themselves became industrialists. The transition from merchant to industrial capitalism was gradual, with significant overlap and continuity.' },
  { q: 'The concept of "economies of scale" helps explain factory production\'s advantage over cottage industry because:', opts: ['Larger factories always paid workers more', 'Centralising production allowed fixed costs (machines, buildings, management) to be spread over larger output, reducing cost per unit', 'Small cottage producers were inherently lazy and inefficient', 'Factory production always produced better quality goods'], ans: 1, exp: 'Economies of scale mean that as output increases, the average cost per unit falls because fixed costs (expensive machinery, factory buildings, management) are spread across more units. This gave large factories a cost advantage over cottage producers that increased over time as better machines enabled still larger efficient scales.' },
  { q: 'Why did the handloom weaving industry survive in India despite the introduction of power looms?', opts: ['The colonial government protected handloom weavers from competition', 'Power looms were banned in India until independence', 'Handlooms could produce finer varieties (especially for ceremonial use), adapt quickly to fashion changes, and operate without the capital investment power looms required', 'Handloom cloth was always cheaper than mill-made cloth'], ans: 2, exp: 'Handlooms persisted because: they could produce fine, patterned textiles (sarees with complex borders, silk brocades) that power looms struggled with initially; they required minimal capital; they could operate on small orders without minimum runs; and they preserved regional design traditions valued by consumers. The handloom sector actually grew in certain segments even as mill production expanded.' },
  { q: 'The East India Company initially traded in India primarily by purchasing textiles. How did this relationship change after the Battle of Plassey (1757)?', opts: ['The EIC became a more equal trading partner with Indian merchants', 'The EIC withdrew from textile trade to focus on military activities', 'Trade became more beneficial to Indian weavers after 1757', 'The EIC used political power to force Indian weavers to sell at dictated low prices and prevented them from trading with other merchants'], ans: 3, exp: 'After Plassey, the EIC transformed from a trading company into a territorial power. It used political muscle to: appoint "gomasthas" (paid agents) who directly supervised weavers, set artificially low purchase prices, prevented weavers from selling to other merchants, and created a monopsony that destroyed the bargaining power of Indian artisans.' },
  { q: 'The "gomastha" system introduced by the East India Company in Bengal affected Indian weavers by:', opts: ['Creating a coercive supervision system where weavers were trapped in debt, forced to sell at low prices, and physically punished for delays', 'Providing advance payments at fair rates and protecting weavers from exploitation by local merchants', 'Organising weavers into cooperatives for better market access', 'Training weavers in new techniques to improve quality'], ans: 0, exp: 'The gomasthas were EIC agents who replaced the local merchant intermediaries. They advanced money to weavers creating debt bondage, forced weavers to sell exclusively to the EIC at below-market prices, physically punished late deliveries, and systematically exploited their captive position — converting independent artisans into essentially bonded workers.' },
  { q: 'What was the significance of the first cotton mill in Bombay established by Cowasji Nanabhai Davar in 1854?', opts: ['It was entirely funded by British capital and staffed by British technicians', 'It was the first Indian-owned large-scale mechanised textile mill, marking the beginning of Indian industrial capitalism in cotton', 'It produced only high-quality luxury fabrics for export to Britain', 'It was established by the colonial government as a pilot project'], ans: 1, exp: 'Davar\'s Bombay Spinning and Weaving Company (1854) was the first steam-powered cotton mill in India, established with Indian (specifically Parsi merchant) capital. It marked the beginning of factory capitalism in India, demonstrating that Indians could organise modern industrial enterprise despite colonial structural disadvantages.' },
  { q: 'Why did industrial production in Britain initially concentrate in regions like Lancashire (cotton) and Yorkshire (wool) rather than being evenly distributed?', opts: ['These regions had the largest populations in Britain', 'The government directed industrial development to these regions', 'These regions had pre-existing craft traditions, available river power, coal deposits (for steam), and port access for importing raw cotton and exporting finished goods', 'These regions had the most fertile agricultural land that supported industrial workers\' food needs'], ans: 2, exp: 'Industrial geography reflected historical crafts (Lancashire\'s textile tradition), natural resources (Pennine rivers for water power, nearby coal for steam), transport access (Manchester\'s canal links to Liverpool port for cotton imports), and agglomeration economies — once industries concentrated, supporting services (machinery makers, banks, merchants) followed, creating self-reinforcing industrial districts.' },
  { q: 'The NCERT chapter emphasises that technology alone did not cause industrialisation. What other factors were equally important?', opts: ['Purely technological invention was the sole driver', 'Religious changes in Protestant Britain were the primary cause', 'Industrialisation was inevitable in all societies given enough time', 'Access to colonial markets and raw materials, merchant capital, labour migration, proto-industrial foundations, and political-institutional frameworks were equally crucial'], ans: 3, exp: 'The textbook emphasises a multi-causal explanation: technology was important but also required cheap raw materials from colonies (American cotton, Indian indigo), colonial markets for surplus output, merchant capital financing factories, workers dispossessed from land migrating to cities, proto-industrial networks providing skills, and Britain\'s institutional advantages (property rights, patent system, parliamentary state supporting industry).' },
  { q: 'A distinctive feature of early British industrialisation was the central role of women in certain industries. In which industry was female labour most extensively employed and why?', opts: ['Cotton textiles — because women\'s nimble fingers suited tasks like spinning, piecing, and textile finishing, and they accepted lower wages', 'Iron and steel — because women were physically stronger', 'Coal mining (above ground) — because safety laws excluded men from surface work', 'Shipbuilding — because maritime law required gender balance'], ans: 0, exp: 'Women were extensively employed in cotton textile mills (as spinners, weavers, and piecers — repairing broken threads), in lace making, and in domestic textile production. Their employment reflected their lower wages (often 50% of male wages), their exclusion from formal craft guilds, and industry preferences for "nimble-fingered" and supposedly more docile workers.' },
  { q: 'The phrase "dark Satanic Mills" from William Blake\'s poem "Jerusalem" (c. 1808) captures which aspect of early industrialisation?', opts: ['The use of coal giving factories dark exteriors', 'The dehumanising, exploitative, and spiritually deadening nature of factory work in early industrial capitalism', 'A celebration of industrial progress and modernity', 'The literal use of Satan as a symbol by Luddite protesters'], ans: 1, exp: 'Blake\'s "dark Satanic mills" is an enduring cultural critique of early industrial capitalism — the factories that trapped workers in repetitive, soul-crushing labour, long hours in dangerous conditions, separated from nature and family, under impersonal capitalist discipline. The phrase encapsulates the Romantic critique of industrial modernity\'s human costs.' },
  { q: 'Which of the following statements about labour migration in early Indian industrialisation is MOST accurate?', opts: ['Workers willingly migrated from rich agricultural regions to industrial cities', 'Indian factory owners recruited workers from Britain to provide technical skills', 'Industrial cities like Bombay drew workers primarily from poor agricultural districts facing debt, land loss, and peasant distress', 'Workers migrated from urban artisan classes to industrial factories voluntarily'], ans: 2, exp: 'The workforce of Bombay\'s mills came largely from Ratnagiri district (Konkan coast) and other drought-prone, debt-ridden agricultural regions. Peasant distress — caused by revenue demands, moneylender debt, loss of land — pushed rural workers to seek wage labour in industrial cities, creating a "reserve army" of labour that kept wages low.' },
  { q: 'The "Charkha" (spinning wheel) promoted by Gandhi during India\'s independence movement can be understood as:', opts: ['A purely technological solution to improve textile quality', 'An attempt to modernise cottage industry through improved machinery', 'Simply a cultural nostalgia for pre-industrial life with no political significance', 'A political symbol of India\'s economic self-sufficiency and resistance against British deindustrialisation, reclaiming the dignity of hand-spinning that colonialism had destroyed'], ans: 3, exp: 'Gandhi\'s promotion of the charkha was profoundly political: it symbolised swadeshi (self-reliance), resistance to British textile imports that had destroyed Indian weavers\' livelihoods, dignity of manual labour, and economic self-sufficiency. The spinning wheel became the emblem of the Indian National Congress and appeared on the Swaraj flag — transforming a humble tool into a revolutionary symbol.' },
  { q: 'What was the primary reason the British East India Company sought to expand its political control over Indian territories from the 18th century?', opts: ['To control revenue (land tax) to finance its armies and trading operations, creating a self-financing empire', 'Purely missionary motives to spread Christianity', 'To protect Indian merchants from French competition', 'To build railways and infrastructure as a gift to Indians'], ans: 0, exp: 'The EIC\'s territorial expansion was driven by the search for revenue. Military campaigns were expensive, and controlling territories provided land revenue to finance armies and further conquests. Bengal\'s revenues (after Plassey) effectively funded the EIC\'s military operations across South Asia — creating a self-financing imperial expansion machine.' },
  { q: 'How did the introduction of railways in India affect Indian handloom weavers in a negative way?', opts: ['Railways had no negative impact on weavers', 'Railways massively accelerated the penetration of cheap British machine-made cloth into previously isolated interior markets, destroying local weaving industries', 'Railways helped weavers by improving access to better raw cotton', 'Railways mainly hurt British merchants, not Indian weavers'], ans: 1, exp: 'Before railways, interior India was partially protected from British cloth by high inland transport costs. Railways shattered this protection: Manchester cloth could now be shipped cheaply to Lahore, Lucknow, or Madras, undercutting local weavers who had previously supplied these markets. The very infrastructure of "development" thus accelerated deindustrialisation.' },
  { q: 'In the context of early industrialisation, "capital accumulation" refers to:', opts: ['Collecting historical artefacts in museums', 'Government tax collection to fund public infrastructure', 'The process by which profits from trade, agriculture, or earlier industries are reinvested to finance new productive enterprises like factories', 'The growth of national debt through government borrowing'], ans: 2, exp: 'Capital accumulation — the reinvestment of surplus/profit into productive assets — is the engine of capitalism. In Britain, capital accumulated through: colonial trade profits, slave trade and plantation profits, agricultural "enclosure" of common lands, and profits from putting-out systems. This accumulated capital financed the expensive machinery and buildings of the Industrial Revolution.' },
  { q: 'The NCERT text notes that industrialists often preferred "cheap and controllable" labour over expensive machinery, especially in labour-abundant countries like colonial India. What does this tell us about industrialisation?', opts: ['Industrialisation always follows the same path regardless of labour conditions', 'Cheap labour always produces better quality goods than machines', 'Colonial industrialisation was always more advanced than metropolitan industrialisation', 'The choice between labour and capital-intensive production depends on relative factor prices; where labour is cheap, mechanisation is delayed'], ans: 3, exp: 'Economic logic: if labour is abundant and cheap (colonial India) while machinery is expensive and imported, rational capitalists minimise machinery and maximise use of cheap labour. This explains why Indian factories remained more labour-intensive and less mechanised than British ones even in similar industries — the different factor price ratios shaped different technological choices.' },
  { q: 'The Bombay cotton mill industry initially focused on coarse yarn and cloth rather than fine textiles. What was the reason for this strategic choice?', opts: ['Indian mills could compete with Lancashire in coarse cloth (using cheaper Indian labour) but not fine cloth where Lancashire had superior technology and skills', 'Indian consumers preferred low quality cloth', 'The colonial government mandated that Indian mills only produce coarse fabric', 'Indian raw cotton was suitable only for coarse production'], ans: 0, exp: 'Indian mills\' competitive advantage lay in coarse yarn and cloth: they could undercut Lancashire using cheap local labour for basic spinning and weaving. In fine cloth, Lancashire had superior ring-spinning technology, skilled workers, and established quality reputations that Indian mills initially could not match. Competitive strategy thus concentrated in market segments where cost advantages mattered most.' },
  { q: 'What was the significance of the "second industrial revolution" (c. 1870–1914) for global manufacturing?', opts: ['It was identical to the first Industrial Revolution', 'It introduced new industries (steel, chemicals, electrical equipment, internal combustion engines) and new powers (Germany, USA, Japan) challenging British industrial supremacy', 'It was led exclusively by Asian nations including India', 'It had no significant impact outside Britain'], ans: 1, exp: 'The second industrial revolution saw new science-based industries (chemicals, electrical engineering, steel) in which Germany and the USA surpassed Britain. New production methods (Bessemer steel, chemical synthesis) and new consumer industries (automobiles, electrical goods) emerged. Germany and the USA became the industrial leaders of the 20th century, ending British industrial hegemony.' },
  { q: 'Workers in early British factories faced the problem of "paternalism" from factory owners. This meant:', opts: ['Factory owners gave generous bonuses and benefits to workers', 'Paternalism refers to the government\'s role in supporting industries', 'Factory owners expected deference, obedience, and gratitude from workers in exchange for employment, housing, or small welfare provisions — creating a dependent, controlled workforce', 'Workers managed factories in a fatherly way'], ans: 2, exp: 'Factory paternalism involved owners providing tied housing, company stores, Sunday schools, or sick clubs in exchange for workers\' complete compliance and deference. This created dependency relationships and was partly designed to prevent workers from unionising or seeking better conditions elsewhere. It combined limited welfare with social control.' },
  { q: 'The Chartist movement of the 1830s–1840s in Britain was significant in the context of industrialisation because:', opts: ['It was purely an economic movement demanding higher factory wages', 'It succeeded in immediately winning all its demands through parliamentary action', 'It was led by factory owners demanding protection from foreign competition', 'It was the first mass working-class political movement demanding democratic rights (universal male suffrage, secret ballot) as a remedy for industrial exploitation'], ans: 3, exp: 'Chartism represented industrial workers\' realisation that political power was necessary to remedy economic exploitation. The six-point Charter (1838) demanded: universal male suffrage, secret ballot, no property qualification for MPs, payment of MPs, equal constituencies, and annual parliaments. Chartism failed immediately but its demands were eventually achieved, showing how industrialisation generated democratic pressures.' },
  { q: 'In the context of Indian industrialisation, the "Managing Agency" system functioned by:', opts: ['Firms that managed multiple industrial enterprises for commission, providing capital, business connections, and management services that individual entrepreneurs lacked', 'Government agencies managing state-owned enterprises', 'British officials managing Indian firms on behalf of the EIC', 'Trade unions managing factories during strikes'], ans: 0, exp: 'Managing agencies were financial-commercial firms that promoted, financed, and managed multiple industrial companies for a management fee and commission. They pooled capital from investors, leveraged their commercial networks and credit, and professionally managed enterprises that individual investors could not run alone. They were central to both British and Indian industrial development.' },
  { q: 'The phrase "capitalism needs workers who have nothing to sell but their labour" reflects which social transformation linked to industrialisation?', opts: ['The invention of wage labour as a concept by industrialists', 'The dispossession of peasants and artisans from land and tools, forcing them to become wage workers entirely dependent on factory employment', 'The voluntary choice of workers to prefer factory wages to subsistence farming', 'A Marxist utopia where workers own factories'], ans: 1, exp: 'Marx\'s concept of "primitive accumulation" describes how pre-capitalist producers (peasants with land, artisans with tools and skills) were dispossessed through enclosure of commons, colonialism, guild destruction, and deindustrialisation — creating a proletariat with "nothing to sell but their labour power." This dispossession was the social prerequisite for factory capitalism.' },
  { q: 'Early British industrialists faced a "labour discipline problem" in the first factories. What did this mean?', opts: ['Workers were physically incapable of doing factory work', 'Factory machinery was unreliable creating unpredictable work schedules', 'Workers accustomed to agricultural or craft rhythms resisted the clock-based, continuous discipline of factory work and required coercive management methods to conform', 'British workers preferred night shifts that made management difficult'], ans: 2, exp: 'Pre-industrial workers followed "task orientation" — working intensely to complete a task then stopping. Factory production required continuous, clock-disciplined, pace-regulated labour subordinated to machine rhythms. This cultural transformation — imposing factory time-discipline on workers accustomed to irregular work patterns — required extensive coercion, fines, bells, clocks, and eventually internalisation of industrial time.' },
  { q: 'Which of the following best explains why power looms did NOT immediately replace handlooms in 19th-century Britain?', opts: ['Power looms were technically inferior to handlooms in all respects', 'The government subsidised handlooms to protect traditional industry', 'Power loom workers consistently went on strike, preventing adoption', 'Power looms were initially better for basic weaving; handlooms retained advantage in complex patterns, fine thread counts, and small customised orders for luxury markets'], ans: 3, exp: 'The transition from handloom to power loom was gradual (1830s–1850s in Britain) because: early power looms had tension and thread-breakage problems with fine yarn; handlooms were better for intricate patterns and high-end fabrics; and weaver wages initially fell (rather than rising) as abundant hand-weavers competed with machines, paradoxically slowing mechanisation.' },
  { q: 'The experience of the Lancashire cotton workers during the US Civil War (1861–65) illustrates which aspect of global interdependence?', opts: ['The "Cotton Famine" — when Union blockade cut Southern cotton exports — caused massive unemployment in Lancashire mills, showing how deeply global supply chains affected distant workers\' lives', 'European workers were unaffected by events in America', 'American industrialisation had surpassed British cotton by 1861', 'Lancashire workers actively supported the Confederacy to restore cotton supplies'], ans: 0, exp: 'The Union blockade of Confederate ports during the Civil War cut off American cotton to Lancashire mills, causing the "Cotton Famine" (1861–65): over 300,000 workers were unemployed and starving in Lancashire. Paradoxically, many Lancashire workers supported the Union cause despite personal suffering, showing remarkable internationalist solidarity against slavery.' },
  { q: 'What role did the "patent system" play in Britain\'s early Industrial Revolution?', opts: ['It prevented inventors from benefiting from their inventions by requiring free sharing', 'It gave inventors time-limited monopolies on their inventions, creating incentives to invest in innovation while eventually releasing knowledge publicly', 'It was copied directly from Chinese invention protection systems', 'Patents were irrelevant as most inventors were motivated by curiosity, not profit'], ans: 1, exp: 'Britain\'s patent system (strengthened by the Statute of Monopolies, 1624) gave inventors 14-year monopolies on their inventions. This created economic incentives to invest in research and development — Watt\'s long patent (extended to 1800) allowed him to recoup R&D costs. The system balanced innovation incentives with eventual public access to new technologies.' },
  { q: 'The "truck system" (or tommy shop system) in early British industry was a form of worker exploitation that involved:', opts: ['A transport system for moving goods between factories', 'A military-style management approach in factories', 'Paying workers in goods at company-owned stores rather than cash, at inflated prices that reduced effective wages', 'A cooperative system where workers shared factory profits'], ans: 2, exp: 'The truck system involved paying workers in tokens or credit redeemable only at company-owned "tommy shops" (truck shops), which charged inflated prices. This allowed employers to recapture a portion of wages paid out, effectively reducing real wages. The Truck Acts (1831 onward) tried to ban the system, requiring payment in cash.' },
  { q: 'Why does the NCERT text emphasise the role of merchants over technology in explaining industrialisation?', opts: ['Technology was unimportant compared to trade networks', 'Merchants invented all the key industrial technologies', 'The text actually emphasises technology over merchants', 'Merchant capital, trade connections, and market development preceded and enabled technological innovation — technology responded to economic opportunities created by expanding trade and colonial markets'], ans: 3, exp: 'The NCERT\'s analytical framework stresses that technological invention was stimulated by economic opportunity: expanding colonial and domestic markets created demand for more output, which incentivised labour-saving machinery. Merchant capital financed both the proto-industrial phase and early factories. Without trade networks and accumulated capital, isolated technological invention would have had limited impact.' },
  { q: 'What happened to the "skilled" handloom weavers of Britain in the period 1820–1850?', opts: ['They experienced catastrophic wage collapse as power looms expanded, reducing their earnings by 75–80% and forcing many into acute poverty before the profession eventually disappeared', 'They were reabsorbed into the cotton mills as skilled machine operators', 'They successfully lobbied Parliament for protection from power looms', 'They emigrated to India and the United States where handloom demand remained high'], ans: 0, exp: 'British handloom weavers\' real wages collapsed from perhaps 25–30 shillings per week in 1800 to 5–6 shillings by the 1830s as expanding power loom production drove down piece rates. The weavers could not compete but also could not easily find alternative employment. Millions experienced grinding poverty during this transition — one of the great social catastrophes of the Industrial Revolution.' },
  { q: 'In early Indian industrial centres like Bombay, the "jobber" (also called mukaddam) played what critical role?', opts: ['A government-appointed factory inspector ensuring labour laws were followed', 'A trusted old worker who recruited labour from his home village, settled workers in the city, and acted as intermediary between management and workforce — wielding significant informal power', 'A skilled engineer responsible for maintaining factory machinery', 'A merchant who supplied raw cotton to mill owners at fixed prices'], ans: 1, exp: 'The jobber (mukaddam) was a crucial figure in early Indian industrialisation: he recruited workers from his home region (often the same village/caste network), helped them find housing, advanced loans, settled disputes, and acted as a buffer between mill management and workers. This gave jobbers immense informal authority — and opened avenues for exploitation through kickbacks and favours.' },
];

// CH13_SOCIAL (History Ch5 — Print Culture and the Modern World)
const SOCIAL_CH13 = [
  { q: 'Woodblock printing technology was developed in China and had spread to Europe by the early 15th century. What was its primary limitation compared to later printing technologies?', opts: ['Each page required a separately hand-carved wooden block, making it labour-intensive and inflexible for producing varied texts in large quantities', 'It could only print in black ink with no possibility of colour', 'It produced inferior quality images compared to hand-copying', 'Woodblock printing was exclusive to imperial use and unavailable to private printers'], ans: 0, exp: 'Woodblock printing required carving a separate block for every page of text. While efficient for high-demand texts (Buddhist sutras, calendars) that were printed repeatedly, it was uneconomical for varied texts in smaller print runs. Movable type overcame this by enabling blocks to be rearranged and reused for different texts.' },
  { q: 'China developed the world\'s first system of printing. In which century was woodblock printing first used in China for books?', opts: ['2nd century CE', '6th century CE', '10th century CE', '13th century CE'], ans: 1, exp: 'China began printing books using woodblocks around the 6th century CE (Tang Dynasty period), with the Diamond Sutra (868 CE) being one of the oldest surviving printed books. Chinese printers later developed ceramic movable type (Bi Sheng, c. 1040 CE) centuries before Gutenberg\'s metal movable type in Europe.' },
  { q: 'Johann Gutenberg\'s printing press, developed around 1448 in Mainz, Germany, represented a breakthrough primarily because:', opts: ['It was the first printing technology in human history', 'It was sponsored by the Catholic Church to spread religious uniformity', 'It used durable metal movable type that could be rapidly recomposed for different texts, enabling fast, cheap, and large-scale reproduction of manuscripts', 'It could print in multiple colours simultaneously'], ans: 2, exp: 'Gutenberg\'s innovation was adapting screw-press technology (used for olive and wine presses) to printing using metal movable type that was durable, uniform, and reusable. His alloy (lead, tin, antimony) created type that could withstand thousands of impressions. A book that took months to hand-copy could now be printed in days, at a fraction of the cost.' },
  { q: 'The Gutenberg Bible, printed around 1455, is significant because:', opts: ['It was the first book ever written', 'It was distributed free by the Catholic Church to promote literacy', 'It contained significant textual errors that discredited the printing press initially', 'It was the first substantial book printed using movable metal type in Europe, demonstrating the viability of mechanised printing for complex texts'], ans: 3, exp: 'The Gutenberg Bible (c. 1455) was the first major book printed with metal movable type in Europe, produced in an edition of around 180 copies. It demonstrated the press\'s capacity for high-quality production of complex texts, launching the era of mechanical reproduction of books. Surviving copies are among the world\'s most valuable books.' },
  { q: 'The printing press spread rapidly across Europe after 1448. By 1500, approximately how many books had been printed in Europe?', opts: ['Approximately 20 million', 'Approximately 10,000', 'Approximately 100,000', 'Approximately 2 million'], ans: 0, exp: 'By 1500, European presses had produced approximately 20 million copies of books (incunabula). This explosion of printed material — representing centuries\' worth of manuscript copying — radically transformed the availability of knowledge, reduced book prices, and began reshaping European intellectual and cultural life.' },
  { q: 'Martin Luther\'s 95 Theses (1517) gained unprecedented reach compared to earlier church criticisms primarily because:', opts: ['Luther personally toured all of Europe distributing copies', 'The printing press enabled rapid reproduction and distribution of his critique of Church abuses across German-speaking lands within weeks', 'The Catholic Church accidentally helped distribute Luther\'s theses', 'Luther\'s Latin was uniquely simple and accessible to all readers'], ans: 1, exp: 'Earlier critics of Church corruption (Wycliffe, Hus) had limited reach because manuscripts spread slowly. Luther\'s 95 Theses, nailed to Wittenberg church door in Latin in October 1517, were quickly translated into German and printed in their thousands. Within weeks, copies had spread across German-speaking Europe — the printing press made Luther\'s Reformation possible in a way earlier reformers\' could not replicate.' },
  { q: 'The printing press\'s role in the Protestant Reformation illustrates which principle about media technology?', opts: ['New media technologies always support existing power structures', 'The Church was unable to use print to defend itself', 'New media can empower challengers to established authority by enabling rapid, cheap, and wide dissemination of alternative viewpoints', 'Print technology affected religious life but had no political consequences'], ans: 2, exp: 'The Reformation demonstrates how new media empower challengers: Luther used print to bypass Church control of information and reach popular audiences directly. The Church initially failed to contain the challenge because it could no longer monopolise the production and dissemination of religious texts. Eventually both sides used print, creating a religious "print war" that transformed European Christianity.' },
  { q: 'Benedict Anderson\'s concept of "print capitalism" and "imagined communities" argues that:', opts: ['Capitalism used print media purely to advertise products', 'Nations existed before print and were merely described by newspapers', 'Only capitalist nations could develop print culture', 'Print capitalism created national languages, national reading publics, and the sense of belonging to a nation of strangers — the imagined community of the nation — enabling modern nationalism'], ans: 3, exp: 'Anderson\'s "Imagined Communities" (1983) argues that the nation is imagined (members will never meet most fellow nationals) but real in its cultural effects. Print capitalism created vernacular reading publics who, reading the same newspapers and novels simultaneously, developed a sense of shared identity — the foundation of modern nationalism. Print standardised languages and created the cultural infrastructure of nationhood.' },
  { q: 'What was a "chapbook" in 18th-century Britain, and what social function did it serve?', opts: ['A cheap, pocket-sized pamphlet sold by pedlars containing popular stories, ballads, religious tales, and practical advice — making print accessible to ordinary, semi-literate people', 'An expensive illustrated book for wealthy collectors', 'Official government documents distributed through churches', 'Academic publications by early universities'], ans: 0, exp: 'Chapbooks were cheap, often crudely printed pamphlets (typically 8–24 pages) sold by chapmen (pedlars) at fairs and markets for a penny or two. They contained popular ballads, crime stories, almanacs, religious tales, and political satire. Chapbooks extended print culture to semi-literate working people who could not afford proper books, creating a popular print culture.' },
  { q: 'The rise of the novel as a literary form in 18th-century Europe is closely connected to which social development?', opts: ['The invention of the printing press in the 15th century directly caused the novel', 'The growth of a literate middle class with leisure time to read, print\'s capacity to produce cheap long texts, and lending libraries that made novels accessible', 'Novels were invented to replace religious texts in Protestant countries', 'The novel emerged in oral culture before being written down'], ans: 1, exp: 'The 18th-century novel (Defoe, Richardson, Fielding) required: a reading public with leisure (urban middle class), affordable printed books, and lending libraries that allowed non-buyers to read. The novel in turn shaped the reading public — its epistolary and realistic forms created new modes of interiority, empathy, and individual subjectivity that were distinctively modern.' },
  { q: 'The Catholic Church attempted to control printing through the "Index Librorum Prohibitorum." What was this?', opts: ['A catalogue of recommended books for Catholic readers', 'A censorship board that reviewed all manuscripts before printing', 'A list of books forbidden to Catholics, maintained by the Church to prevent the spread of heretical, dangerous, or immoral printed material', 'A licensing system requiring all printers to obtain Church approval'], ans: 2, exp: 'The Index of Forbidden Books (established 1559 by Pope Paul IV) listed books Catholics were prohibited from reading on pain of excommunication. It included works by Copernicus, Galileo, Erasmus, and Protestant reformers. The Index acknowledged print\'s power by the very scale of the Church\'s response — it survived until 1966.' },
  { q: 'Pre-revolutionary France had a complex system of book censorship. Despite this, how did forbidden books circulate?', opts: ['They were printed legally in France using bribed officials', 'They circulated only in manuscript form, as printing was too risky', 'The French censorship system was entirely effective, preventing forbidden books from reaching readers', 'They were printed in neighbouring countries (Switzerland, Netherlands) and smuggled into France, or circulated in underground networks of booksellers and reading clubs'], ans: 3, exp: 'French royal censorship could not stop forbidden books: publishers operated just across the border in Amsterdam, Geneva, and other cities, then smuggled books across French frontiers. A thriving underground book trade (libri clandestini) distributed Enlightenment texts, Jansenist tracts, pornography, and political satire. The "forbidden best-sellers" of pre-revolutionary France suggest censorship actually increased demand.' },
  { q: 'Enlightenment philosophers like Voltaire and Rousseau used print to attack which institutions?', opts: ['The Catholic Church\'s authority, absolute monarchy, superstition, and traditional social hierarchies — championing reason, individual rights, and empirical inquiry', 'Only the colonial administrations of France', 'Protestant churches but supported Catholic institutions', 'Only scientific institutions they disagreed with'], ans: 0, exp: 'Voltaire\'s wit demolished religious intolerance and Church authority; Rousseau\'s social contract questioned monarchical legitimacy; the Encyclopédie (Diderot and d\'Alembert) systematically undermined religious superstition through empirical knowledge. These ideas, spread through print, are credited with contributing to the intellectual climate that produced the French Revolution of 1789.' },
  { q: 'James Augustus Hickey\'s Bengal Gazette, established in 1780, was significant because:', opts: ['It was the first newspaper in Asia and was controlled by the colonial government', 'It was the first newspaper in India — a privately owned, English-language paper that was provocative, satirical, and critical of the East India Company\'s administration', 'It was the first vernacular language newspaper in India', 'It supported the EIC\'s policies and helped spread British colonial values'], ans: 1, exp: 'Hickey\'s Bengal Gazette (or Calcutta General Advertiser, 1780) was India\'s first newspaper. Hickey used it to attack the EIC administration and publish gossip about officials. The government confiscated his press and imprisoned him, illustrating both print\'s potential as a medium of dissent and colonial authorities\' alarm at uncontrolled printing in India.' },
  { q: 'The Vernacular Press Act of 1878 in India was designed to:', opts: ['Encourage the growth of Indian-language newspapers by providing subsidies', 'Establish a regulatory body for quality control of vernacular press content', 'Severely restrict the freedom of Indian-language newspapers to criticise British rule, empowering the government to seize presses of offending publications without trial', 'Require all newspapers to be published in both English and vernacular languages'], ans: 2, exp: 'The Vernacular Press Act (1878), pushed by Viceroy Lytton, allowed magistrates to demand bonds from vernacular newspaper owners and seize presses and materials of papers deemed seditious — without judicial review. It was specifically designed to silence criticism of British rule in Indian-language papers, which were reaching a rapidly growing educated readership.' },
  { q: 'Raja Ram Mohan Roy\'s newspaper "Sambad Kaumudi" (1821) was significant in Indian reform history because:', opts: ['It was the first English-language newspaper in India', 'It was published by the East India Company to spread English education', 'It exclusively covered news of the Bengal Presidency\'s administrative decisions', 'It was a Bengali newspaper that advocated reform of Hindu practices (opposing sati, advocating widow remarriage) and criticised British policies — using print as a tool of social reform'], ans: 3, exp: 'Sambad Kaumudi ("Moon of Intelligence") was Roy\'s Bengali weekly that campaigned against sati, supported widow remarriage, advocated women\'s education, and critiqued both orthodox Hinduism and colonial policies. Roy pioneered using the vernacular press as a vehicle for social reform — demonstrating print\'s potential for progressive transformation of Indian society.' },
  { q: 'Bal Gangadhar Tilak\'s newspaper "Kesari" (founded 1881) played what role in the Indian nationalist movement?', opts: ['It was a fiercely nationalist Marathi newspaper that denounced British rule and mobilised popular anti-colonial sentiment, earning Tilak imprisonments for sedition', 'It was a moderate publication supporting British administrative reforms', 'It was devoted exclusively to religious and cultural topics', 'It advocated economic cooperation with British manufacturers'], ans: 0, exp: 'Kesari ("Lion" in Marathi) was Tilak\'s vehicle for aggressive nationalism. He used it to celebrate Indian history and culture, criticise colonial policies, and arouse mass national consciousness. His articles on the Plague Commission and famine policies led to sedition charges and imprisonment. Kesari reached tens of thousands of Marathi readers and was central to making nationalism a mass movement.' },
  { q: 'How did the printing press affect the status of Latin as the language of European intellectual and religious life?', opts: ['Print reinforced Latin\'s dominance as the language of all printed books', 'Print accelerated the rise of vernacular languages (French, German, English, Spanish) as the primary languages of print, marginalising Latin and enabling national literary cultures', 'Print preserved Latin exclusively while vernacular languages remained oral', 'The printing press was initially only used for vernacular texts, never Latin'], ans: 1, exp: 'While early print (including the Gutenberg Bible) used Latin, publishers quickly discovered that vernacular-language texts had much larger markets. Luther wrote in German to reach ordinary people; French, English, and other vernacular literatures flourished in print. This gradually made vernacular languages the primary vehicles of intellectual life, standardising national languages and creating national literatures.' },
  { q: 'The concept of "oral culture" versus "print culture" is significant because:', opts: ['Oral cultures were entirely replaced by print cultures after the printing press', 'Print culture was always superior to oral culture in transmitting knowledge accurately', 'The two coexisted and interacted — print incorporated oral forms (ballads, sermons, folk tales) while oral transmission continued to be important, especially for the poor and illiterate', 'Print culture only affected elite classes with no impact on popular or oral traditions'], ans: 2, exp: 'Walter Ong and other scholars have shown that print and oral culture were not simply successive stages but complex interacting systems. Oral genres (ballads, sermons, folk tales) were transcribed into print; printed texts were read aloud to illiterate audiences; chapbooks and almanacs mediated between print and oral worlds. The binary is an oversimplification of a more complex cultural ecology.' },
  { q: 'What was the role of "lending libraries" or "circulating libraries" in 18th and 19th-century Europe and India?', opts: ['They were charitable institutions for the poor with no commercial function', 'They functioned as government censorship institutions', 'They were primarily used by scholars and had no role in popular reading', 'They made expensive books accessible to readers who could not afford to buy them, dramatically expanding the reading public beyond those wealthy enough to own books'], ans: 3, exp: 'Lending libraries (commercial in Europe, sometimes reading rooms in colonial India) allowed readers to pay a small subscription to borrow books rather than buying them. This dramatically lowered the effective cost of reading, expanding the reading public into the middle and even lower-middle classes and making novels, periodicals, and non-fiction widely accessible.' },
  { q: 'How did print culture contribute to women\'s social reform movements in 19th-century India?', opts: ['Women\'s magazines, advice literature, and reform tracts created a space for debate about women\'s rights, education, and social reform, while literate women began writing about their own experiences', 'Print reinforced conservative patriarchal values and opposed women\'s education', 'Print had no significant impact on women\'s social position in colonial India', 'Only British women benefited from print culture in India'], ans: 0, exp: 'Women\'s journals (like Stri Dharma Neeti), reform tracts on women\'s education, and novels depicting women\'s lives created new publics discussing women\'s status. Women like Tarabai Shinde, Pandita Ramabai, and Rashsundari Devi used writing to articulate women\'s experiences and demands. Literacy itself became a contested site — some reformers promoted it, conservatives feared educated women would become "uncontrollable."' },
  { q: 'Rashsundari Devi\'s autobiography "Amar Jiban" (1876) is significant in Indian print history because:', opts: ['It was the first novel published in India', 'It is considered the first autobiography written in Bengali by a woman, describing her secret self-education and the social constraints on women — a landmark in women\'s writing and print culture', 'It was a political manifesto for women\'s suffrage', 'It was a religious text criticising Hindu rituals'], ans: 1, exp: 'Rashsundari Devi\'s Amar Jiban (My Life, 1876) is a landmark: written by a high-caste Bengali housewife who secretly taught herself to read using torn pages, it is the first known Bengali autobiography by a woman. It candidly describes domestic confinement, the pain of female illiteracy, and the transformative power of literacy — a powerful testimony to what print culture meant for women.' },
  { q: 'The spread of print culture in colonial India had a paradoxical effect on the caste system. How?', opts: ['Print uniformly promoted caste equality and social reform', 'Print had no significant impact on caste dynamics', 'Print both challenged caste (by spreading Ambedkar\'s and Phule\'s critiques) and reinforced it (by publishing caste-specific journals, caste histories claiming Kshatriya status, and religious texts reifying caste boundaries)', 'Only upper castes benefited from print, with lower castes entirely excluded'], ans: 2, exp: 'Print\'s impact on caste was contradictory: Jyotirao Phule used print to attack Brahmin dominance; Ambedkar\'s Mooknayak and Bahishkrit Bharat championed Dalit rights. Simultaneously, upper-caste communities published caste journals and genealogies claiming Kshatriya lineage, and religious printing reinforced orthodox practices. Print could serve both liberation and entrenchment depending on who controlled it.' },
  { q: 'The "almanac" was one of the most widely printed and read texts in early modern Europe. This tells us that:', opts: ['Most early readers were scholars interested in astronomy', 'Almanacs were banned by the Church as competing with religious calendars', 'Only wealthy merchants used almanacs for business planning', 'Practical knowledge (agricultural calendars, weather predictions, auspicious dates, medical advice) was the primary concern of the popular reading public, not literary or philosophical texts'], ans: 3, exp: 'Almanacs were annual publications combining astronomical data (moon phases, eclipses) with agricultural guidance, weather forecasts, astrological predictions, medical advice, and religious festivals. They were among the best-selling printed texts because they provided practical information ordinary people genuinely needed. Their popularity shows that "functional literacy" for practical purposes preceded literary reading for most people.' },
  { q: 'The French Revolution of 1789 is connected to print culture in which of the following ways?', opts: ['Enlightenment ideas spread through print created a reading public critical of absolute monarchy; revolutionary journalism (newspapers, pamphlets, political cartoons) mobilised popular opinion and spread revolutionary ideas nationwide', 'The Revolution had no connection to print culture as most French people were illiterate', 'Print culture was used exclusively by royalists to defend the monarchy', 'The Revolution banned all printing as a tool of aristocratic power'], ans: 0, exp: 'Print was central to the Revolution: the Encyclopédie and Philosophes created intellectual foundations challenging divine-right monarchy; underground libelles (satirical pamphlets) destroyed royal and aristocratic reputations; Jacobin newspapers like L\'Ami du Peuple mobilised sans-culottes; printing presses in Paris and provinces spread revolutionary laws, declarations, and propaganda nationally. The Revolutionary period saw an explosion of newspaper titles.' },
  { q: 'In colonial India, why did the British colonial authorities fear the vernacular press more than the English-language press?', opts: ['The English press was better regulated and more professional', 'Vernacular papers reached the far larger Indian-language reading public, including newly literate lower-middle classes; nationalist criticism in vernacular languages could mobilise mass audiences that English-language papers could not reach', 'English papers were pro-nationalist while vernacular papers were conservative', 'There was no significant difference in how authorities treated the two presses'], ans: 1, exp: 'By the 1870s–1880s, newspapers in Bengali, Marathi, Hindi, Urdu, and other vernaculars were reaching thousands of readers among the educated lower-middle classes — teachers, clerks, small merchants — who were becoming politically conscious. This mass nationalist print public was more threatening to colonial rule than English-language papers reaching a smaller, more "manageable" elite readership.' },
  { q: 'Which of the following best describes the impact of print culture on religious diversity in Reformation Europe?', opts: ['Print enabled one universal Christian church to emerge', 'Print was only used by Catholics to defend orthodoxy against Protestants', 'Print fragmented Christianity by enabling multiple denominations to publish their own Bibles, catechisms, and theological texts — making religious uniformity impossible to maintain', 'Print had no significant impact on European religious life'], ans: 2, exp: 'Before print, the Church could maintain orthodoxy by controlling manuscript production. Print broke this monopoly: Lutherans, Calvinists, Anabaptists, and Catholics all published their own texts, creating competing religious reading publics. Each denomination developed its own print culture — hymnals, catechisms, vernacular Bibles — that permanently fragmented European Christianity and made religious coexistence necessary.' },
  { q: 'What was the significance of printing in vernacular languages (rather than Latin) for the development of nationalism?', opts: ['Vernacular printing had no impact on nationalism as only educated people read', 'Vernacular printing reinforced regional diversity, actually preventing national unity', 'Only Latin printing could create national consciousness because of its universal educated readership', 'Printing in vernacular languages created unified linguistic communities within national territories — standardising spelling and grammar, creating national literary canons, and enabling "print-mediated" national identities'], ans: 3, exp: 'Anderson\'s argument: print capitalism standardised vernacular languages (choosing one dialect as standard spelling/grammar), creating national linguistic communities. Germans reading Luther\'s German Bible developed a sense of shared German-ness; the French reading the same novels and newspapers developed French identity. Standardised vernacular print is a prerequisite for modern nationalism.' },
  { q: 'The "print-capitalism" argument holds that newspapers were crucial to nationalism because:', opts: ['Newspapers created a daily ritual where millions of strangers simultaneously read the same text, developing awareness of their national community — the "ceremony" of consuming the same news creating imagined national solidarity', 'Newspapers published only government propaganda that created patriotism', 'Only newspapers with large circulations affected nationalist sentiment', 'Newspapers were too expensive for most people to read, limiting their nationalist impact'], ans: 0, exp: 'Anderson\'s insight: the newspaper reader, seeing millions of others reading the same text simultaneously (though unseen), experiences the imagined community of the nation. The newspaper\'s daily temporal rhythm — published, read, and discarded together — creates a shared national "nowness" that reinforces national identity as simultaneous, horizontal community.' },
  { q: 'How did the printing press affect the Catholic Church\'s authority in Europe?', opts: ['Print strengthened the Church by enabling mass distribution of orthodox religious texts', 'Print undermined Church authority by enabling individuals to read the Bible directly, facilitating Protestant challenges, spreading criticism of clerical corruption, and breaking the Church\'s monopoly on religious knowledge', 'The Church successfully used print censorship to prevent all challenges to its authority', 'Print had no significant impact on Church authority as religious experience was primarily oral'], ans: 1, exp: 'Print was devastating for Church authority\'s traditional monopoly: vernacular Bible printing enabled lay readers to interpret scripture directly, bypassing priests; Luther\'s pamphlets reached millions criticising clerical corruption; scientific works challenged Church cosmology; underground satirical texts mocked clerical hypocrisy. The Index and Inquisition could not contain print — the Reformation and eventual secularisation of European culture were partly products of this.' },
  { q: 'The development of print culture in India was shaped by which specific colonial context that differed from European print history?', opts: ['India had no oral tradition that could interact with print culture', 'Print in India simply replicated European patterns with no significant differences', 'Colonial print culture involved languages and communities defined by colonial rule, with censorship serving imperial rather than dynastic interests, and with print being used both as a tool of colonial control and anti-colonial resistance simultaneously', 'The Indian printing press was controlled entirely by Christian missionaries with no Indian participation'], ans: 2, exp: 'Indian print culture had distinctive features: missionary presses introduced printing partly to translate religious texts and "civilise" Indians; colonial authorities used print for administrative control; Indian reformers and nationalists used print for social reform and anti-colonial mobilisation; caste, religion, language, and colonial power intersected in complex ways that had no direct parallel in European print history.' },
  { q: 'Jyotirao Phule\'s use of print and writing in 19th-century India can be described as:', opts: ['Using print to reinforce Brahminical Hindu orthodoxy', 'Publishing government documents in Marathi for administrative purposes', 'Writing exclusively on agricultural topics with no social commentary', 'Using Marathi writing (Gulamgiri, Sarvajanik Satyadharma) to attack Brahmin dominance, challenge caste hierarchy, and articulate a radical vision of social equality based on a critique of Hindu textual authority'], ans: 3, exp: 'Phule (1827–1890) used print as a weapon against caste oppression: Gulamgiri (Slavery, 1873) compared Brahmin exploitation to American slavery; he exposed the Aryan invasion theory to argue that so-called low castes were original inhabitants oppressed by Brahmin settlers. Phule\'s print activism is a model of how subordinated groups used emerging print culture to challenge dominant ideologies.' },
  { q: 'What does the textbook mean when it discusses "the reading fury" (Lesewut) in 18th-century Germany?', opts: ['Both — a phenomenon of rapid expansion of reading culture AND simultaneous moral panic by elites fearing that widespread reading of "dangerous" romantic novels and political texts would corrupt morals and destabilise society', 'A German social panic about people reading too many dangerous books', 'A government programme to expand literacy through state schools', 'A religious movement using Bible reading to combat Enlightenment atheism'], ans: 0, exp: 'The Lesewut (reading mania/fury) describes the explosion of reading in late 18th-century Germany as literacy spread and cheap printed texts multiplied. It had two dimensions: the genuine hunger for printed texts among newly literate classes, AND the moral panic of conservatives and clergy who feared that romantic novels, political pamphlets, and irreligious texts were corrupting the newly literate masses — a recurrent tension when new reading publics emerge.' },
  { q: 'How did the printing press change the relationship between author and text?', opts: ['Authors lost all connection with their texts once printed', 'Print created the concept of authorship as intellectual property (enabling copyright), fixed the text (reducing the fluid variation of manuscript culture), and gave authors wider audiences but also vulnerability to misrepresentation of their ideas', 'The printing press had no significant impact on the concept of authorship', 'Authors gained complete control over their texts through print censorship mechanisms'], ans: 1, exp: 'Print transformed authorship: the fixed, reproducible text created the concept of a definitive author\'s text; copyright law (emerging from the Statute of Anne, 1710) gave authors legal ownership of their intellectual property; but print also meant ideas spread rapidly beyond authors\' control, could be taken out of context, and authors became subject to public criticism (and censorship) in entirely new ways.' },
  { q: 'The slogan "the pen is mightier than the sword" reflects which historical observation about print culture?', opts: ['Physical weapons are less effective than propaganda in warfare', 'Print was used exclusively by pacifists who opposed all violence', 'Ideas spread through print can be more powerful than military force in transforming societies — print-enabled reformations, revolutions, and national movements changed the world more profoundly than many military campaigns', 'The phrase refers specifically to literary merit surpassing military history'], ans: 2, exp: 'Bulwer-Lytton\'s phrase (1839) captures a genuine historical observation: the printed word spread ideas (Reformation, Enlightenment, nationalism, socialism) that transformed entire civilisations in ways that military conquest could not. Luther with his printing press did more to transform European Christianity than any army; enlightenment pamphlets prepared the French Revolution more than any military plot.' },
  { q: 'The newspaper "Al-Hilal" founded by Maulana Abul Kalam Azad in 1912 illustrates which aspect of print and nationalism in colonial India?', opts: ['Print was exclusively used by Hindu nationalists', 'Al-Hilal supported British colonial rule and opposed the independence movement', 'Urdu newspapers had negligible readership and political impact', 'Muslims used Urdu print culture to articulate both Islamic identity and anti-colonial nationalism — showing that print served multiple communities in building nationalist consciousness in their own vernaculars'], ans: 3, exp: 'Al-Hilal (The Crescent) in Urdu reached a Muslim readership and combined Islamic religious reform with sharp anti-colonial nationalist politics. It was suppressed by the British in 1914. It illustrates that Indian nationalism was not monolithic — it expressed itself through multiple vernacular print traditions, each addressing their communities\' specific religious and cultural identities within a broader anti-colonial framework.' },
  { q: 'The "penny press" revolution in 19th-century America and Britain transformed journalism by:', opts: ['Making newspapers affordable to working-class readers through mass production, advertising revenue, and sensational content — creating a mass-circulation popular press that widened the public sphere dramatically', 'Producing expensive quality journalism for elite subscribers', 'Publishing exclusively government-approved content for social stability', 'Focusing exclusively on financial news for the business community'], ans: 0, exp: 'The penny press (from the 1830s in the US, 1850s in Britain) used steam-powered presses and advertising revenue to sell newspapers for 1 penny (vs. 6 cents for subscription papers) to working-class mass audiences. Sensational crime stories, human interest pieces, and popular politics replaced the dry commercial and political content of elite papers, fundamentally democratising access to news.' },
  { q: 'What was the role of the printing press in the spread of scientific knowledge during the Scientific Revolution (16th–17th centuries)?', opts: ['The Church\'s censorship prevented scientific works from being printed', 'Print enabled rapid pan-European dissemination of scientific discoveries, allowed scientists to build on each other\'s work, created an international scientific community, and standardised scientific notation and terminology', 'Scientific knowledge was transmitted exclusively through university lectures without print', 'Print was used for scientific works only after the Industrial Revolution'], ans: 1, exp: 'Print transformed science: Copernicus\' De Revolutionibus (1543), Vesalius\' anatomical atlas (1543), and Newton\'s Principia (1687) reached hundreds of scholars across Europe rapidly. Print created a "republic of letters" — an international scientific community exchanging ideas through journals (like the Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society, 1665). Standardised printed texts enabled scientific replication and cumulative knowledge-building.' },
  { q: 'In the context of print and nationalism, how did the standardisation of language through print contribute to the formation of national identities?', opts: ['Language standardisation always reflected natural linguistic evolution independent of print', 'All languages were equally preserved and promoted through print', 'Print capitalism required choosing one dialect as the standard form, creating unified national linguistic communities from diverse regional dialects and marginalising minority languages within nation-states', 'Language standardisation was imposed by governments independently of printing technology'], ans: 2, exp: 'Print publishers chose the dialect with the largest potential market as their standard — Luther\'s Bible standardised High German; Parisian French became the French national standard; the King James Bible shaped standard English. These choices were commercial decisions with profound political consequences: they created national language communities while marginalising speakers of "minority" or "dialect" variants.' },
  { q: 'How did print culture affect the nature and speed of political communication during colonial India\'s nationalist movement?', opts: ['Political communication remained slow and limited to urban elites even with print', 'Political parties used print only for internal communication, not public mobilisation', 'Print culture was equally accessible to colonial authorities and nationalists, giving neither a significant advantage', 'Print accelerated political mobilisation: nationalist newspapers, pamphlets, and leaflets spread ideas to new educated classes, coordinated activities across geographically distant regions, and created a shared nationalist discourse impossible before mass printing'], ans: 3, exp: 'Print transformed Indian nationalism\'s organisational capacity: Congress session resolutions could be reprinted and discussed across Bengal, Bombay, and Madras simultaneously; Gandhi\'s speeches reached millions through newspapers; Tilak\'s Kesari mobilised Marathi readers; Subramania Bharati\'s poetry in Tamil inspired a generation. Print created a pan-Indian nationalist public from linguistically diverse, geographically dispersed communities.' },
  { q: 'The first printing press in India was brought by Portuguese missionaries in the 16th century. What was its initial purpose?', opts: ['Printing religious texts (Bible translations and Christian literature) in local languages to aid conversion of Indians to Christianity', 'Printing commercial trade documents for Portuguese merchants', 'Printing administrative laws for colonial governance', 'Printing scientific texts to educate Indian scholars in European science'], ans: 0, exp: 'Jesuit missionaries brought a printing press to Goa in 1556, primarily to print translations of the Bible, Christian catechisms, and religious tracts in local languages (Konkani, Tamil) for the purpose of converting Indians. This made India\'s first printing press an instrument of religious imperialism, though its technology would later be used to challenge colonial and religious authority.' },
  { q: 'How did the "Swadeshi movement" of 1905 use print culture as a tool of resistance?', opts: ['It banned all English-language newspapers and replaced them with Indian-language ones', 'Swadeshi activists used vernacular newspapers, pamphlets, and songs to spread the boycott of British goods and promote Indian manufactures — making print a weapon of economic nationalism', 'Print was not used in the Swadeshi movement which relied on oral communication only', 'Swadeshi leaders used only English-language press to reach educated audiences who could influence policy'], ans: 1, exp: 'The Swadeshi movement (triggered by the Partition of Bengal, 1905) saw an explosion of nationalist print: vernacular newspapers spread boycott lists and Swadeshi product advertisements; nationalist songs and poems were printed and circulated; journals explained the economic logic of self-reliance. Print was central to building the mass mobilisation that made Swadeshi more than an elite protest.' },
  { q: 'Which 19th-century European development enabled mass production of cheap newspapers — making print genuinely accessible to working classes for the first time?', opts: ['The discovery of abundant paper-making forests in colonial territories', 'Government subsidies to newspaper publishers across Europe', 'The development of rotary steam-powered printing presses, cheap wood-pulp paper replacing expensive rag paper, and linotype typesetting machines — all lowering production costs dramatically', 'The abolition of literacy requirements for reading newspapers'], ans: 2, exp: 'Three innovations transformed newspaper economics: rotary steam presses (printing on continuous rolls at high speed), cheap wood-pulp paper from the 1870s (replacing costly rag paper), and linotype machines (1886) enabling fast typesetting. Together they slashed production costs, allowing penny/halfpenny papers to reach mass working-class readerships that previously had no access to daily news.' },
  { q: 'The concept of "censorship" has existed as long as print. Which of the following best describes how pre-modern (pre-1800) censorship of print typically worked?', opts: ['Governments employed AI systems to scan all printed texts', 'Censorship was entirely ineffective and never succeeded in suppressing any ideas', 'Censorship was only applied to scientific texts that contradicted religious beliefs', 'Through pre-publication licensing (requiring government approval before printing), post-publication seizure, burning of books, and prosecution of authors and printers for sedition or heresy'], ans: 3, exp: 'Pre-modern censorship operated through multiple mechanisms: licensing systems requiring official approval before printing (England\'s Licensing Act to 1695); heresy trials prosecuting authors and printers; public burning of books; and imprisonment or death for seditious publication. Despite these, determined publishers could always find ways to print and distribute — censorship stimulated underground print networks rather than eliminating dangerous ideas.' },
  { q: 'The development of the "novel" as a literary form in 18th and 19th-century India differs from its European context in which important way?', opts: ['The Indian novel emerged in dialogue with both traditional narrative forms (Sanskrit tales, oral epics) and the European novel, creating hybrid forms that addressed colonial experience, caste, and social reform', 'Indians had no interest in fictional narratives before European contact', 'Indian novels were merely translations of European novels with no original content', 'The Indian novel developed simultaneously with the European novel in the 18th century'], ans: 0, exp: 'The Indian novel (emerging in Bengali, Marathi, Hindi, Tamil from the 1860s–80s) was neither a simple copy of the European form nor entirely indigenous. Writers like Bankim Chandra Chattopadhyay combined European novelistic techniques with Sanskrit narrative traditions, indigenous social themes (caste, gender, colonialism), and nationalist concerns, creating a distinctively Indian modern literary form.' },
  { q: 'B.R. Ambedkar\'s use of print media (Mooknayak, 1920; Bahishkrit Bharat, 1927) was significant for which reason?', opts: ['He used print exclusively to appeal to upper-caste reformers for support', 'He used print to create a Dalit public sphere — building Dalit consciousness, challenging Brahminical interpretations of texts, and organising political resistance among previously voiceless communities', 'Ambedkar\'s newspapers were banned immediately and had no significant readership', 'He used English-only publications that could not reach ordinary Dalit readers'], ans: 1, exp: 'Ambedkar\'s newspapers were revolutionary acts: they created communication among geographically dispersed Dalit communities, challenged the Brahminical monopoly on interpreting religious texts, reported atrocities that upper-caste papers ignored, and built political consciousness for the Dalit movement. "Mooknayak" (Leader of the Voiceless) captured his mission — print gave voice to the historically silenced.' },
  { q: 'What was the significance of the "Gutenberg parenthesis" — the suggestion that print culture (1450–2000) was historically exceptional rather than the norm for human communication?', opts: ['The Gutenberg parenthesis argues that print was always the dominant medium throughout human history', 'The parenthesis theory argues that Gutenberg\'s press was historically insignificant', 'It suggests that pre-print oral/manuscript culture and post-print digital culture share characteristics (non-linear, participatory, interconnected) that print\'s fixed, authoritative, linear text temporarily displaced', 'It refers to the period between Gutenberg\'s birth and death'], ans: 2, exp: 'The Gutenberg Parenthesis (Thomas Pettitt) argues that print culture\'s characteristics — fixed authoritative texts, linear reading, clear authorship, stable knowledge — were a historical exception between oral/manuscript culture and digital culture. The internet (like oral culture) features participatory, non-linear, remixed, and unstable texts — suggesting print\'s authority was temporary. This theory helps understand digital disruption of traditional media.' },
  { q: 'Mahatma Gandhi\'s newspaper "Young India" (English) and "Navajivan" (Gujarati/Hindi) were significant because:', opts: ['They were commercial newspapers focused on business news', 'They were government-approved publications that worked within colonial press censorship', 'They focused exclusively on religious topics without political content', 'They served as platforms for Gandhi\'s political philosophy — spreading ideas of non-violence, Swaraj, and Swadeshi to both English-educated elites and vernacular-reading masses simultaneously'], ans: 3, exp: 'Gandhi used his newspapers as integral tools of political mobilisation: Young India addressed English-educated opinion-makers globally while Navajivan reached a mass Gujarati and Hindi readership. He wrote prolifically, explaining satyagraha philosophy, responding to political events, and building the ideological framework of the independence movement — making print inseparable from his politics.' },
  { q: 'The relationship between print culture and oral culture in colonial India was best described as:', opts: ['A complex symbiosis — printed texts were read aloud to illiterate audiences, oral traditions were transcribed into print, and performance traditions (drama, kirtan, tamasha) incorporated printed scripts and nationalist songs', 'Completely separate — print replaced oral culture entirely in literate communities', 'Hostile — oral culture actively resisted the spread of print literacy', 'Print had no connection to oral traditions which continued entirely independently'], ans: 0, exp: 'In colonial India, literacy was low but print\'s reach extended far beyond literate readers: newspapers and pamphlets were read aloud in tea shops, bazaars, and village gatherings; nationalist songs were printed and taught orally; printed plays and tamasha scripts were performed for mass illiterate audiences. Print and orality were deeply intertwined in creating mass political consciousness.' },
  { q: 'What does the phrase "print is the technology of individualism" (attributed to Marshall McLuhan) mean in historical context?', opts: ['Print made everyone selfish and antisocial', 'The fixed, private, silent reading of print created a new experience of individualism — the reader alone with the text, internalising ideas privately — contrasting with communal oral reception', 'Print technology was invented by individual geniuses rather than communities', 'Print was only used by wealthy individuals, not collective institutions'], ans: 1, exp: 'McLuhan argued that print\'s linear, fixed, silent-reading format created a new mode of individual consciousness: unlike communal oral or performance culture, the private reader internalises ideas alone, developing personal interpretation and interiority. This "typographic man" — rational, individualist, linear — is a product of print culture, contrasting with the communal, multi-sensory experience of oral societies.' },
];
